A||||12||Voyd||how do u replace the headliner?||||5
Z||000000||Voyd||11-21-1999||02:56 PM||||my headliner is old, worn, stained and sagging at places. i want to change it presto. i just have no idea how to go about it. does anyone have detailed explanations about how to do this operation? <P>thanks||||reg||5
Z||000001||Gokart||11-21-1999||03:12 PM||||Try this. Got it from Fiero secrets.http://www.castlegate.net/personals/daveheld/TechArt/headlinr.htm I would like to know myself what glue to use so it won't blled through and show from the outside?! The glue described in this procedure is for the entire headliner itself and not just sagging material coming off the foam part.<p>[This message has been edited by Gokart (edited 11-21-1999).]||||reg||2
Z||000002||theogre||11-21-1999||04:36 PM||||The headline uses a foam backed fabric on a semi ridgid pannel. You pretty much have to replace the whole mess with new foam backed headliner material. Gluing the fabric to the foam isn't likely to work.||||reg||1
Z||000003||fiero56||11-21-1999||06:12 PM||||Hey Voyd,<BR>Go here: <A HREF="http://www.surf.to/FIERO_OSG" TARGET=_blank>http://www.surf.to/FIERO_OSG</A> <BR>Click "Interior", then "Headliner Removal".<BR>It explaines everything you need to do to replace it, plus they tell you how to restore they fabric and what type of glues work for this applicaiton.<p>[This message has been edited by fiero56 (edited 11-21-1999).]||||reg||10
Z||000004||Monkeyman||11-21-1999||11:57 PM||||Here's another site I found.  <A HREF="http://members.xoom.com/_XMCM/WPFC/headliner.html." TARGET=_blank>http://members.xoom.com/_XMCM/WPFC/headliner.html.</A>   (Don't put the last period on the end.  I was just ending a sentence.)  Although it is possible to use original headliner material, have you though about using something different?  I'm working on a custom interior, and I'm looking for a good man-made leather (can't find any thin enough that don't have that shiny, plastic look, though).  Look in your local fabric shop for ideas.  Just a thought.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Carrolles||11-22-1999||12:19 AM||||I bought foam backed headliner material from a fabric store (Hancock) for about $7.50/yard.  I bought 1-1/2 yards so I'd have enough to recover the sun visors.<P>I used 3M Foamfast 74 (I also got this at the fabric store).  it sprays on like silly string and does not soak through the fabric. <P>When you get the headliner out of the car strip off the old fabric and use a broom to sweep off all the old deteriorated foam.  Spray the glue on the headliner board and on the back of the fabric.  It works kind of like contact cement only you have about ten minutes to adjust the fabric and smooth out wrinkles before the glue sets.  Be sure and cut the fabric a little larger than the headliner board to allow for it to wrap around the edges.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Racer 1||11-22-1999||12:57 AM||||I did my own also. Got foam back headliner material and High Temp trim adhesive cost about $20.00 took me about a day to do it I havee T-Tops. I just ripped the old liner out and glued this stuff straight to the bare metal. Looks good just place towels down in your fiero so the glue doesnt get on anything.<BR> <A HREF="http://fierorob.homepage.com/" TARGET=_blank>http://fierorob.homepage.com/</A> ||||reg||6
Z||000007||DaRkLoRD||11-22-1999||01:17 AM||||Monkeyman, do you have any of your custom interior done yet? If so, I'd love to see some pics. I've got a few ideas for a custom interior as well, but nothing built yet.||||reg||1
Z||000008||ggarsk||11-22-1999||07:29 AM||||Voyd,if your not in to big of a hurry I'll have a headliner to get rid of soon. I just redone it. I'm going to put a sun roof in so it will be no good to me. It's not a big problem to replace one,just be careful when pulling of the trim so you don't break the plastic clips that hold it on.||||reg||1
Z||000009||Voyd||11-22-1999||09:50 AM||||thanks alot guys. the fabric and the process of putting it on is not the problem, i have someone who is highly competent in all things upholstery. just wanted to know how to take it out without breaking anything  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000010||pollock||11-22-1999||09:54 AM||||The ONLY way to do a headliner right is to take it to a shop.  If you want to save money, remove the board yourself and just have them cover it.  Done that way, it might cost $35.  The glue they use is far superior, and the material will be correct.<P>Otherwise, you could spend 15-20 on material yourself, another 5-10 on glue, spend 2 hours doing the work, and end up with a headliner that looks like somebody's first try.  <P>Monkeyman, there is a brand of vinyl called Allante that looks very much like leather.  Usually only a pro can spot it.  See your local upholstery shop for details.||||reg||14
Z||000011||Monkeyman||11-22-1999||08:27 PM||||Thanks, pollock.  I'm going to the upholsterers tomorrow.  I'll ask him about it.||||reg||1
Z||000012||Dave85||11-23-1999||07:33 PM||||I've replaced my headliner with "alternative material" and I must say it came out EXCELLENT!!!!  I used a type of fleece material from Hancocks Fabrics. The adhesive I used was 3M Super Trim Adhesive #08090.  I would recommend having an assitant help with the removal and then when reinstalling.  I plan on doing Mr. Mikes seats, visors and carpet.||||reg||1
